{% set local_problem %}
<p>New Hope's terrain creates unique downspout challenges. Sloped lots mean water moves fast and aggressively. If downspouts dump water in the wrong spot, it rushes downhill — potentially toward your foundation rather than away from it.</p>

<p>Many homes in this area have inadequate downspout systems — undersized, too few, or positioned without considering the terrain. When heavy rain hits, these systems can't keep up, and water ends up exactly where you don't want it.</p>

<p>Proper downspout design accounts for your property's slope, soil conditions, and drainage patterns. Done right, water flows away from your home and exits at a safe location.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has capacity, positioning, and extensions working together.</p>

<p>Capacity means enough downspouts, properly sized. We often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts for North Alabama's heavy rainfall.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally collects and where discharge makes sense for your terrain. Extensions or underground drains then carry water to safe exit points.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been solving drainage problems in eastern Madison County for over 20 years. We understand sloped lots, clay soil, and the importance of working with terrain rather than against it.</p>

<p>Our solutions are designed for your specific property, not generic approaches.</p>
{% endset %}

